.Dd $Mdocdate: August 5 2026 $
.Dt XSUNABA 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm Xsunaba
.Nd isolate X11 applications with Xephyr and unveil
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar command Op Ar argument ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility provides a two-layer sandbox for running X11 GUI applications
on
.Ox :
.Bl -enum -compact -offset indent
.It
Display isolation via a nested
.Xr Xephyr 1
X server, which prevents the application from reading X events
(keystrokes, mouse input, clipboard) intended for other windows in the
parent X session.
.It
Filesystem restriction via
.Xr unveil 2 ,
which limits the filesystem paths available to the application process.
This layer is configured through an environment variable.
.El
.Pp
When invoked from the command line,
.Nm
starts Xephyr on an unused display (default
.Ql :32
\-
.Ql :99 ) ,
generates an X11 magic cookie for authentication, forks the target
application with user-specified unveil restrictions, waits
for it to exit, then stops Xephyr and cleans up the authentication
cookie.
.Pp
.Nm
can also be loaded as a Perl module via
.Cm require
to incorporate pledge or unveil functionality into custom scripts,
either with or without the Xephyr display layer.
.Pp
The
.Xr OpenBSD::Pledge 3p
interface used by this Perl implementation configures only the current
process and does not expose the
.Fa execpromises
argument of
.Xr pledge 2 .
An executed program would therefore start without those restrictions.
.Nm
does not claim to pledge arbitrary target applications and rejects a
non-empty legacy
.Ev XSUNABA_PLEDGE
setting.
The low-level
.Fn pledge
module function remains available for restricting Perl code itself.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "XSUNABA_UNVEIL"
.It Ev XSUNABA_UNVEIL
Comma-separated list of
.Ar path : Ar perm
entries passed to
.Xr unveil 2
for the sandboxed application.
.Pp
When unset, the application inherits the launcher's filesystem view
and no additional unveil restrictions are applied (the filesystem is
fully visible).
When set, only the listed paths, the private X authority file, and the
X11 socket directory are accessible after unveil is locked;
all other paths become invisible.
.Pp
Permission codes are single-letter abbreviations that may be combined:
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width "rwxc" -compact -offset indent
.It r
Read files, list directories.
.It rx
Read and execute.
.It rw
Read and write existing files.
.It rwx
Read, write, and execute existing files.
.It rwc
Read, write, and create new files.
.It rwxc
Read, write, execute, and create (full access).
.El
.Pp
Example:
.Ql /usr/X11R6/bin/xterm:rx,/tmp:rwc,/etc:r,/dev:r .
.Pp
The private X authority file and
.Pa /tmp/.X11-unix
socket directory are additionally unveiled with
.Ql r
and
.Ql rw ,
respectively.
.Pa /dev
is not exposed automatically; list only the devices the application needs.
.It Ev XSUNABA_DISPLAY
Starting X display number for
.Xr Xephyr 1 .
If the socket
.Pa /tmp/.X11-unix/X Ns Ao Ar N Ac
already exists,
.Nm
scans upward from this number to find a free display.
Default:
.Ql :32 .
.It Ev WIDTH
.Xr Xephyr 1
display width in pixels.
Default:
.Ql 1024 .
.It Ev HEIGHT
.Xr Xephyr 1
display height in pixels.
Default:
.Ql 768 .
.It Ev VERBOSE
When set to a non-empty value, emits informational messages to standard
output during setup, application launch, and cleanup.
Debug messages are written to standard error.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/tmp/Xsunaba-xauth-XXXXXX"
.It Pa /tmp/Xsunaba-xauth-XXXXXX
Unique mode-0600 temporary X authority file created for each Xephyr session.
Contains the magic cookie used to authenticate the application
connection.
Its path is passed to the application in
.Ev XAUTHORITY
and it is removed during normal cleanup.
.It Pa /tmp/.X11-unix/
Directory scanned for existing X11 sockets to find a free display
number.
.It Pa /tmp/.Xsunaba-display-UID-N.lock
Per-user advisory lock used to serialize concurrent allocation of display
number
.Ar N .
.It Pa /usr/bin/openssl
Used to generate the X11 magic cookie (128-bit random hex string).
.It Pa /usr/X11R6/bin/xauth
Used to add the X11 authentication cookie.
.It Pa /usr/X11R6/bin/Xephyr
The nested X server executable.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Pp
If no command is provided, the usage message is printed to standard
error and the exit status is 255.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Launch Firefox with filesystem restrictions:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ XSUNABA_UNVEIL="/usr/local/bin/firefox:rx,/usr/local/lib/firefox:rx,/tmp:rwc,/etc:r" \e
  Xsunaba firefox --private-window
.Ed
.Pp
Run xterm with minimal access, verbose output enabled:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ VERBOSE=1 \e
  XSUNABA_UNVEIL="/usr/X11R6/bin/xterm:rx,/tmp:rwc,/etc:r" \e
  Xsunaba xterm
.Ed
.Pp
Use Xsunaba as a Perl module for a custom launcher (no Xephyr):
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#!/usr/bin/perl
require '/usr/local/bin/Xsunaba';

Xsunaba::sandbox(
    app    => 'xterm',
    unveil => [
        '/usr/X11R6/bin/xterm:rx',
        '/tmp:rwc',
        '/etc:r',
    ],
);
.Ed
.Pp
Use the low-level API to restrict the current Perl process:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#!/usr/bin/perl
require '/usr/local/bin/Xsunaba';

Xsunaba::unveil('/usr/share/dict/words', 'r');
Xsunaba::unveil_lock();
Xsunaba::pledge('stdio rpath');

open my $fh, '<', '/usr/share/dict/words' or die "open: $!";
print while <$fh>;
.Ed
.Pp
Override the default promises for the low-level helper:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#!/usr/bin/perl
require '/usr/local/bin/Xsunaba';
$Xsunaba::PLEDGE_PROMISES = 'stdio rpath inet dns';
Xsunaba::pledge();
.Ed
.Pp
Launch a sandbox on a custom display with custom geometry:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ XSUNABA_DISPLAY=":40" WIDTH=1280 HEIGHT=900 Xsunaba gimp
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
Set
.Ev VERBOSE
to a non-empty value to see informational messages:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[INFO] using display :32
[INFO] Xephyr started (PID 12345)
[INFO] launched 'firefox' (PID 12346)
[INFO] stopping Xephyr (PID 12345)
[INFO] cleanup complete
.Ed
.Pp
Warnings are always printed; pledge helper and unveil failures on
.Ox
are fatal.
Errors (always printed) indicate fatal conditions such as
.Ev HOME
or the host
.Ev DISPLAY
not being set, invalid geometry, or Xephyr failing to start.
.Sh MODULE API
.Nm
may be loaded as a Perl module using
.Cm require :
.Pp
.Dl require '/usr/local/bin/Xsunaba';
.Pp
The following functions are exported into the
.Sy Xsunaba
namespace:
.Bl -tag -width "unveil_lock()"
.It Fn pledge $promises
Invoke
.Xr pledge 2
with the given space-separated promise string.
If
.Ar $promises
is
.Dv undef
or omitted, the package variable
.Va $Xsunaba::PLEDGE_PROMISES
is used as the default.
No-op on systems other than
.Ox .
.It Fn unveil $path $perm
Invoke
.Xr unveil 2
for
.Ar $path
with permission
.Ar $perm
(default
.Ql r ) .
Must be called before
.Fn unveil_lock .
No-op on systems other than
.Ox ,
or if unveil is already locked.
.It Fn unveil_lock
Lock the unveil configuration by calling
.Fn unveil
with two
.Dv NULL
arguments.
After this call, the filesystem is restricted to the set of paths
announced with
.Fn unveil ,
and no further unveil calls are permitted.
No-op on systems other than
.Ox .
.It Fn sandbox %opts
Convenience wrapper that applies unveil, then
.Xr exec 3 Ns s
the application directly (no
.Xr Xephyr 1 ) .
Accepts the following keys:
.Bl -tag -width "unveil" -compact -offset indent
.It Cm app
Executable to run (required).
.It Cm args
Array reference of command-line arguments.
.It Cm unveil
Array reference of
.Ql path:perm
strings.
.It Cm lock
Boolean controlling whether
.Fn unveil_lock
is called automatically.
Defaults to true when
.Cm unveil
entries exist.
.El
.It Fn launch %opts
Full sandbox launcher: starts
.Xr Xephyr 1 ,
forks the application with optional unveil restrictions, waits for the
application to exit, then stops Xephyr and removes the X11
authentication cookie.
Accepts the following keys:
.Bl -tag -width "display" -compact -offset indent
.It Cm app
Executable to run (required).
.It Cm args
Array reference of command-line arguments.
.It Cm display
X display number (default
.Ql :32 ) .
.It Cm width
Xephyr width in pixels (default
.Ql 1024 ) .
.It Cm height
Xephyr height in pixels (default
.Ql 768 ) .
.It Cm unveil
Array reference of
.Ql path:perm
strings.
.El
.El
.Ss Package variables
.Bl -tag -width "PLEDGE_PROMISES"
.It Va $Xsunaba::PLEDGE_PROMISES
Default pledge promise string used by
.Fn pledge
when no explicit promise string is provided.
Default:
.Ql stdio rpath wpath cpath fattr proc exec inet dns unix tty .
Set this variable before calling
.Fn pledge
to override its default.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr openssl 1 ,
.Xr perl 1 ,
.Xr xauth 1 ,
.Xr Xephyr 1 ,
.Xr pledge 2 ,
.Xr unveil 2 ,
.Xr exec 3 ,
.Xr X 7
